---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import site from '../../../site.config.mjs';
const n = site.name;
---
<Base title="About" crumbs={[{ label: 'about' }]}>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1 class="page-title">About {n}</h1>
    <p>
      {n} is a personal archive of research papers by {site.author}. It is laid out like a preprint server, but it hosts
      only the author's own work and has no moderation, submission or review process. It is not affiliated with arXiv or
      Cornell University.
    </p>

    <h2 id="vibe">Vibe research</h2>
    <p>
      The papers here are produced through <em>vibe research</em>: the questions, the key ideas and the final judgement
      are human, while much of the exploration, computation, checking and writing is done together with AI systems. Each
      abstract page has a section <strong>How this paper was made</strong> that states, as far as the author records it,
      who did what, which agent systems and models were used, how the results were checked, and where supporting
      materials (code, data, transcripts) can be found.
    </p>

    <h2 id="verification">Verification marks</h2>
    <table class="kv legend">
      <tr><th scope="row">Machine-verified</th><td>The results were checked by AI systems (for example independent AI review of the manuscript or of the derivations). This is not formal verification.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Human-verified</th><td>The author has checked the results by hand.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Formally verified</th><td>The main results have been checked in a proof assistant (for example Lean).</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p>
      <span class="mark mark-yes">✓</span> yes &nbsp; <span class="mark mark-partial">◐</span> partially &nbsp;
      <span class="mark mark-no">✗</span> no &nbsp; <span class="mark mark-na">–</span> not stated.
      These marks are the author's own statements. Readers should check every claim independently before relying on it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="identifiers">Identifiers</h2>
    <p>
      Every paper has a permanent identifier <code>{n}:YYMM.NNNN</code>. YYMM is the year and month of the first version
      (UTC) and NNNN is its order within that month. A specific version is cited as <code>{n}:YYMM.NNNNvN</code>; the
      identifier without a version always points to the latest version.
    </p>
    <table class="kv">
      <tr><th scope="row">Abstract page</th><td><code>/abs/YYMM.NNNN/</code> (latest), <code>/abs/YYMM.NNNNvN/</code> (version N)</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">PDF</th><td><code>/pdf/YYMM.NNNN.pdf</code> (latest), <code>/pdf/YYMM.NNNNvN.pdf</code> (version N)</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p>These addresses never change. A withdrawn paper keeps its page and identifier, with a withdrawal notice.</p>

    <h2 id="integrity">Versions and integrity</h2>
    <p>
      A posted PDF is never replaced. Any change, however small, is posted as a new version, and the version history on
      each abstract page lists the UTC time, page count and SHA-256 hash of every version. The site is rebuilt from a
      <a href={site.repoUrl}>public Git repository</a>, and the build refuses to publish if any stored PDF no longer
      matches its recorded hash. The commit history of that repository is a second, independent record of when each
      version was added.
    </p>
    <p>To check that a PDF you downloaded is the posted one, compare its hash with the one on the abstract page:</p>
    <pre class="code">shasum -a 256 2611.0001v1.pdf       # macOS / Linux
certutil -hashfile 2611.0001v1.pdf SHA256   # Windows</pre>

    <h2 id="citing">Citing</h2>
    <p>
      Each abstract page has a BibTeX entry. Cite a paper as <code>{n}:YYMM.NNNN</code> with its URL, or add the version
      (<code>vN</code>) to refer to a specific version.
    </p>

    <h2 id="feed">Feed</h2>
    <p>New papers and new versions appear in the <a href="/feed.xml">Atom feed</a>.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
